{"abstract":"Video game designers often view confusion as undesirable, yet it is inevitable, as new players must adapt to new interfaces and mechanics in an increasingly varied and innovative game market, which is more popular than ever. Research suggests that confusion can contribute to a positive experience, potentially motivating players to learn. The state of confusion in video games should be further investigated to gain more insight into the learning experience of play and how it affects the player experience. In this article, we design a study to collect learning-related affects for users playing a game prototype that intentionally confuses the player. We assess the gathered affects against a complex learning model, affirming that, in specific instances, the player experience aligns with the learning experiences. Moreover, we identify correlations between these affects and the Player Experience Inventory constructs, particularly concerning flow experiences.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","Jarma Montoya, Oleg","Scirea, Marco","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828170"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affect","Confusion","FLOW","Learning","Player Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the dynamics of affect during play and the role of confusion","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112428043/CoG_2025_Short_On_the_dynamics_of_affective_states_during_play_and_the_role_of_confusion_4_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828170","ids":["itu-9ce701a3-057e-4657-a53b-4acc1f6f9cac"],"keyword":["affect","confusion","flow","learning","player experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-D808702DF98233A619ACCA0DCA4DB64C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112428043/CoG_2025_Short_On_the_dynamics_of_affective_states_during_play_and_the_role_of_confusion_4_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Vase Schultz","key":"voldenpau","last":"Volden","locid":"7e884581-3d40-42e6-af12-453e052c9c87","name":"Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","orcid":"0009-0009-2177-5482","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Oleg","key":"jarmamontoyapau","last":"Jarma Montoya","name":"Jarma Montoya, Oleg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"scireapau","last":"Scirea","name":"Scirea, Marco","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/cog64752.2025.11114086","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE Conference on Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9ce701a3-057e-4657-a53b-4acc1f6f9cac"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Dynamics of Affect During Play and the Role of Confusion","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828170","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Studies on software tutoring systems for complex learning have shown that confusion has a beneficial relationship with the learning experience and student engagement (Arguel et al., 2017). Causing confusion can prevent boredom while signs of confusion can serve as a signal of genuine learning and as a predecessor for frustration. There is little to no research on the role of confusion in early childhood education and playful learning, as these studies primarily focus on high school and university students during complex learning tasks. Despite that, the field acknowledges that confusion may be caused by inconsistency between information and a student’s internal model referred to as cognitive disequilibrium known from the theory of cognitive development, which was originally theorized based on observational studies on young children (D’Mello and Graesser, 2012). Therefore, there is reason to expect that the virtues of confusion also apply to young children engaging in learning activities, such as playful learning. To investigate the role of confusion in playful learning, we conducted an observational study, in which the behavior and expressed emotions of young children were collected by familiar pedagogues, using a web app, while they engaged with playful learning games designed for kindergartens. The expressed emotions were analyzed using a likelihood metric to determine the likely transitions between emotions (D’Mello and Graesser, 2012). The preliminary results showed that during short play sessions, children express confusion, frustration, and boredom. Furthermore, the observed emotional transitions were matched with previously established models of affect dynamics during complex learning. We argue that games with a learning objective can benefit by purposely confusing the player and how the player’s confusion may be managed to improve the learning experience.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776374"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective Computing","Early childhood education","Playful learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"children s expressed emotions during playful learning games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.34190/ecgbl.18.1.2635","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776374","identifier":[{"id":"85212947972","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-19dd2ef9-15d7-4e0d-907e-b79f6ea87448"],"keyword":["Academic Emotions","Affect Dynamics","Affective Computing","Early Childhood Education","Playful Learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-703B65F51C1030C8B6DF99E8EFB9E89B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.34190/ecgbl.18.1.2635","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.academic-conferences.org/index.php/ecgbl/article/view/2635/2559","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Vase Schultz","key":"voldenpau","last":"Volden","locid":"7e884581-3d40-42e6-af12-453e052c9c87","name":"Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","orcid":"0009-0009-2177-5482","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.34190/ecgbl.18.1.2635","eisbn":["9781917204200"],"isbn":["9781917204194"],"status":"p","title":"European Conference on Game Based Learning","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85212947972"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-19dd2ef9-15d7-4e0d-907e-b79f6ea87448"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Children’s expressed emotions during playful learning games","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776374","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This short paper focuses on procedurally generating rules and communicating them to players to adjust the difficulty. This is part of a larger project to collect and adapt games in educational games for young children using a digital puzzle game designed for kindergartens. A genetic algorithm is used together with a difficulty measure to find a target number of solution sets and a large language model is used to communicate the rules in a narrative context. During testing the approach was able to find rules that approximate any given target difficulty within two dozen generations on average. The approach was combined with a large language model to create a narrative puzzle game where players have to host a dinner for animals that can't get along. Future experiments will try to improve evaluation, specialize the language model on children's literature, and collect multi-modal data from players to guide adaptation.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","Grbic, Djordje","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718832"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Difficulty adjustment","Educational games","Genetic algorithm","Large Language Models","Procedural Generation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"procedurally generating rules to adapt difficulty for narrative puzzle games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.05518","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718832","identifier":[{"id":"85180554237","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1a158d41-175f-41ff-a1b9-08edad2f7bcd"],"keyword":["difficulty adjustment","educational games","genetic algorithm","large language models","procedural generation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-B34EC7EEDC9D83C5801381727E6815BF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/100051811/IEEE_COG_2023_Yoli_Short.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.05518","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"voldenpau","last":"Volden","locid":"7e884581-3d40-42e6-af12-453e052c9c87","name":"Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","name_org":"Volden, Thomas","orcid":"0009-0009-2177-5482","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Djordje","key":"grbicpau","last":"Grbic","locid":"c596539b-3ceb-497c-9a43-e71d6b0f65a6","name":"Grbic, Djordje","orcid":"0000-0002-4388-2539","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2307.05518","eisbn":["9798350322774"],"isbn":["9798350322781"],"pages":"1-4","publisher":"IEEE","series":"Proceedings of the 2023 IEEE conference on Games","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the IEEE Conference on Game"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85180554237"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1a158d41-175f-41ff-a1b9-08edad2f7bcd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Procedurally generating rules to adapt difficulty for narrative puzzle games","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718832","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
